WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.360
A loop looks like a wall of code, but the computer only ever follows one instruction at a time. Keep your eye on the current values, and trace each pass in order.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:22.380
First, one loop. Bay will be one, then two, then three, then four. The if question is asked again on every pass.

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:30.527
Pass one. Bay is one. The if is false, so continue does not run. Display one.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:42.860
Pass two. Bay is two. The if is true. Continue skips display, and jumps to the next bay. This pass is skipped, not ended.

00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:49.433
Pass three. Bay is three. The if is false. Display three.

00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:57.793
Pass four. Bay is four. Display four. Now the loop has no values left, so it ends.

00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:10.653
Now nest the loops. Think of an odometer. Floor is the outer wheel. Bay is the inner wheel. Floor stays fixed while bay runs all the way around.

00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:24.593
Floor is one. Hold that outer value. The inner loop now visits bay one, bay two, bay three, and bay four. Only then may the floor change.

00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:36.233
The inner loop has finished, so floor becomes two. Bay starts again at one, then two, three, and four. The outer loop does not skip ahead.

00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:54.073
Read this code from the outside in. Choose a floor. Then choose a bay. Test if bay is two. Then test the truck condition. Then display the pair. When the pass ends, return to the inner for line for the next bay.

00:01:54.467 --> 00:02:01.987
Floor one, bay one. Bay is not two, and there is no truck. Display one, one.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:12.200
Floor one, bay two. The first if is true. Continue skips display, and the inner loop tries the next bay. Floor is still one.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:18.720
Floor one, bay three. Both ifs are false. Display one, three.

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:29.607
Floor one, bay four. Display one, four. The inner loop is finished, so we go back to the outer for, and floor may change.

00:02:29.633 --> 00:02:36.473
Floor becomes two. The bay loop starts again at one. Display two, one.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:43.260
Floor two, bay two. Continue again. Skip display. Next bay.

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:54.547
Floor two, bay three. The truck condition is true. Print the message. Break ends the bay loop only. Display of two, three never runs.

00:02:54.567 --> 00:03:04.087
Bay four on floor two never starts. Control returns to the floor loop. There is no floor three, so the program stops.

00:03:04.633 --> 00:03:17.233
Continue skips the rest of only this pass, then the same loop tries its next value. Break ends the nearest loop completely. Here that is the bay loop, not the floor loop.

00:03:17.767 --> 00:03:35.367
Now copy the run onto paper as a table. One row for every pass that actually begins. Write floor, then bay, then the condition result, then the action. Do not invent rows for pairs the loop never starts.

00:03:35.833 --> 00:03:44.313
First row. Floor one, bay one. Neither if is true, so the action is display one, one.

00:03:44.733 --> 00:03:58.573
Second row. Floor one, bay two. The bay-equals-two condition is true. The action is continue. The pair is still written, because this pass did begin.

00:03:58.933 --> 00:04:06.813
Third row. Floor one, bay three. Neither condition is true. Display one, three.

00:04:07.233 --> 00:04:16.353
Fourth row. Floor one, bay four. Display one, four. That finishes every inner value for floor one.

00:04:16.733 --> 00:04:26.173
Fifth row. Floor becomes two, and the inner loop starts again. Floor two, bay one. Display two, one.

00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:35.447
Sixth row. Floor two, bay two. Continue again. Write the row. Then move to the next bay.

00:04:35.867 --> 00:04:47.027
Seventh row. Floor two, bay three. The truck condition is true. Write the message, then break. Break is the action on this row.

00:04:46.833 --> 00:04:57.913
Look at what is missing. There is no row for two comma four. Break already left the bay loop, so that pass never begins, and a honest table does not invent it.

00:04:58.467 --> 00:05:08.907
Now count the loops. Start with the outer loop. Floor takes the values one and two, so the outer loop runs two times.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:19.220
If nothing stopped early, each floor would try all four bays. Two floors times four bays is eight possible inner passes.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:30.880
Continue does not remove a pass. Bay two on each floor still began, so those two passes are still counted. Continue only skips the display.

00:05:30.867 --> 00:05:42.707
Break does remove passes. On floor two, break happened at bay three, so bay four never began. Eight minus one gives seven inner passes.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:56.980
Check it against the table. Seven inner passes means seven rows. Two rows were continue, and one row was the break, so the pair display ran seven minus three, which is four times.

00:05:57.533 --> 00:06:12.933
The rhythm is slow and strict. Fix one outer value. Run inner values in order. Test each if. Continue means next inner value. Break means leave the inner loop.
